+// AN ELEMENT EXPECTATION IS A FACT ONLY WHEN IT IS FULLY RESOLVED. A list literal hands its expected
+// element type to every member, and a member that adopts its expectation (a literal, a call whose
+// result is read through it) takes that type as its own; the literal's type is then its first
+// member's. Under `xs: List<T>` with T not yet bound, the element expectation is the generic leaf
+// itself, so the literal typed as List<T> and unify_generics bound nothing from it -- the lambda that
+// later read T saw no type (gunbc.recurring_failure_mode
+// generic_binding_from_a_list_literal_silently_dropped). An unresolved element expectation is
+// therefore not passed down: the members infer their own types and the call's unification binds T
+// from them. The question is asked of the RAW element child as well as of its resolved type:
+// child_type_node reads a TypeVariable-marked leaf through resolved_type, which answers error_type,
+// and an error node reads as resolved -- so asking only the derived node erased the very mark the
+// substitution basis exists to carry. The lambda arm keeps the same discipline for its parameters
+// and declared return.
+fn list_literal_element_expectation(expected: Node?, scope: InferScope) -> Node? {
+  match expected {
+    Present { value: exp } =>
+      if node_is_element_collection(n: exp, source_indices: scope.type_env.source_indices) {
+        match exp.children |> first {
+          Present { value: elem } =>
+            let elem_type = child_type_node(ch: elem)
+            if is_fully_resolved(n: elem, source_indices: scope.type_env.source_indices)
+              && is_fully_resolved(n: elem_type, source_indices: scope.type_env.source_indices) {
+              Present { value: elem_type }
+            } else { none }
+          Absent => none
+        }
+      } else { none }
+    Absent => none
+  }
+}
